Then I changed the parameter values with these combinations:
confidenceFactor	minNumObj	unpruned	subtreeRaising
0.25	            2	        False	    True
0.15	            5	        False	    True
0.35	            1	        False	    False
0.25	            2	        True	    True

confidenceFactor:	Controls pruning. Low = more pruning, simpler tree.
minNumObj:	Minimum instances per leaf. Higher = fewer, bigger leaves.
unpruned:	True = no pruning; False = prune using confidenceFactor.
subtreeRaising:	Adjusts branches during pruning for better generalization.

Pruning: remove parts of the tree that dont improve the classification.

And the best result was the default one, the first combination and the last combination with a 96% of correct guesses.


For the MultilayerPerceptron I used this parameters:
learningRate	momentum	trainingTime	hiddenLayers
0.1	            0.2	        500	            a
0.3	            0.3	        1000	        5
0.2	            0.5	        500	            10
0.1	            0.3	        500	            0

learningRate:	Weight update speed. High = fast but unstable.
momentum:	Helps avoid local minima; smooths weight changes.
trainingTime:	Number of epochs (iterations). More = more learning, longer training.
hiddenLayers:	Number of neurons in hidden layers. More = can learn complex patterns.

And the best result was the third combination with a 98% of correct guesses.



When it comes to the data split I have been using the cross-validation with 10 folds in all the previous models.
But now I used the percentage split with a 75% and a 85% split for training, and the results changed a lot.
With the 75% split I got an accuracy of 89.1892% and with the 85% split an accuracy of 100%.

And using the MultilayerPerceptron I got 97.2973% with the 75% split and 95.4545% with the 85% split.



A confusion matrix shows how many instances of each class were correctly or incorrectly classified.
For the iris dataset (3 classes: setosa, versicolor, virginica):

Diagonal elements: correctly classified instances (True Positives).
Non-diagonal elements: misclassified instances (False Positives/Negatives).

Accuracy is the ratio of correctly classified instances to the total:

Accuracy = True Positives / Total instances


The MultilayerPerceptron worked better in general in every task through the lab work.
MultilayerPerceptron had better accuracy because it can learn non-linear boundaries and feature interactions, allowing it to correctly
classify borderline cases that a decision tree might misclassify.